<worksheet>
  <title>Height of a Yoyo</title>

  <!-- Optional introduction -->
  <objectives>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
               Explore the quadratic function   that models the height of a yoyo as it is dropped and bounces back up.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Use the model to solve quadratic equations.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>
<introduction>
    <p>
      Suppose you have a yo-yo mounted to an 8 foot ceiling (<m>H = 8</m>) initially it is wound up and released at time
<m>t = 0</m>. Then it moves according to <m>H = f(t)</m> where  <md>
    f(t)=t^2-5t+8
</md>
where <m>f(t)</m> is height measured in feet (above the floor) and <m>t</m> is measured in seconds. <m>H = 0</m> corresponds to the
floor.
    </p>
    <p>
        Graph the function above using Desmos. Use the graph to answer the following questions.
    </p>
</introduction>
<exercise workspace="0.25in">
    <p>
      Does the yo-yo hit the floor? How do you know  
    </p>
</exercise>


<exercise workspace="0.25in">
    <p>
      When does the yo-yo return to the ceiling? 
    </p>
</exercise>



<exercise workspace="0.25in">
    <p>
      How many times is it 3 feet from the floor? Can you tell approximately when? 
    </p>
</exercise>

<exercise workspace="0.25in">
    <p>
      How many times is it 1.75 feet from the floor? Can you tell approximately when? 
    </p>
</exercise>


<exercise workspace="0.25in">
    <p>
      How many times is it 0.5 feet from the floor? Can you tell approximately when? 
    </p>
</exercise>

<exercise workspace="0.25in">
    <p>
      When does the yo-yo comes closest to the floor? What is the approximate height? What is the exact
height?
    </p>
</exercise>
  </worksheet>